use /lib
use /ui
from /scope/application/Body import Body
from ./chat-input import ChatInput
from ./message-list import MessageList
from /ui/components/right-sidebar import TasksTab
from ./queue-list import QueueList
from /scope/sandbox import Sandbox
from /scope/harness-integration import HarnessIntegration
from /scope/no-project import NoProjectState
from /scope/open-projects import OpenProjects
from /scope/project-data import ProjectData
from /scope/prompt-mode/ask-conversation import AskConversation
from /scope/prompt-mode/mode-system-prompts import ModeSystemPrompts
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModes
from /scope/prompt-mode/sending import PromptSending
from /ui/components/scrollbar import Scrollbar

export ui-component PromptEditor:
    pitch:
        An interface for sending tasks to a local coding harness like Claude
        Code, written directly with compiled-on-depand Piton.

    description:
        Not a chat. Each prompt sent is a task for the harness, tasks can
        queue, and each task shows its output until it finishes. There are no
        message bubbles.

    basicDesignAndLayout:
        The @{MessageList} fills the body's Chat tab: all the space between
        the body's tab bar and the @{ChatInput} along the bottom. From top to bottom it holds the
        header, holding the latest task and every other task under way, one
        of them selected and shown in full and the rest as compact rows, as
        the @{MessageList} says, and the selected task's output. The previous
        tasks and the queue are in the right sidebar's Tasks tab, as the
        @{TasksTab} says.

        Before any task has been sent, the space shows a hint instead, which
        wraps when the view is narrow. It reads
        "Write a prompt below. Enter adds a line; Ctrl/Cmd+Enter sends it."
        or, while no project is open, as the @{NoProjectState} says,
        "Open a project to start prompting."

    chatHistory: @{MessageList}

    previousTasks:
        Previous tasks are listed in the right sidebar's Tasks tab, in its
        Previous group, as the @{TasksTab} says, every task sent but
        those the header shows, each a row that opens it there, and its
        output in a tab of its own in the body, as the @{Body} says,
        never in the Chat tab. Their heading counts them:
        "No previous tasks", "1 previous task", or "N previous tasks". The
        selection, filters, and batch actions below work there as they say,
        along that group's heading.

        Every task in the list has a checkbox at the left of its heading,
        before its status, and clicking it selects or deselects the task
        without opening or closing it. Shift-clicking one selects every task
        between it and the one last clicked, never deselecting any; with none
        clicked before, it selects or deselects that one as a plain click
        does. The selection is kept while the list is closed and opened again,
        until it is cleared or sent. Each open project keeps its own, as the
        @{OpenProjects} says.

        While any task is selected, the row shows the batch actions at its
        right, in place of nothing: "N selected", muted, counting every task
        selected, then "Send N to Spec" and "Send N to Code", small ghost
        buttons with a swap icon, each tinted the colour of the mode it sends
        to, then "Clear", which deselects them all. Send N to Spec counts, and
        sends, the selected tasks that offer Send to Spec, and Send N to Code
        those that offer Send to Code, as the @{PromptSending} says; each
        is shown only while its count isn't zero. Tasks that don't offer it,
        sent in Chain or Freeform, whose mode isn't known, already sent to the
        other mode or being sent there, marked done, or complete, can be
        selected, and are counted in "N selected", but neither action sends
        them. Clicking an action never opens or closes the list.

        A batch action sends each of its tasks to the other mode, as the
        @{PromptSending} says for one, oldest first: the first starts
        straight away while its lane is free and the rest queue behind it in
        that lane, so they run in the order they were first sent, each saved in the
        history as a new task. Once sent, the tasks it sent are deselected,
        the rest of the selection is kept, and the list stays as it was, still
        expanded, with nothing opened. With no project open, it sends nothing,
        a notification says a project must be opened first, and the selection
        is kept.

    previousTaskFilters:
        The filters are one dropdown, never a bar of chips: a small ghost
        button with a filter icon at the right of the Previous group's
        heading in the Tasks tab, shown while the group holds a task, reading
        "Filter", or, while any filter is on, how many are, as "Filter · 2",
        in the theme's accent. Active, it stands out more than when no
        filter is on, never less: its icon and label are the accent at full
        strength, on a faint tint of the accent behind the button, about a
        sixth of its strength, as a selected ghost button is, readable in
        light and dark mode, never dimmed, faded, or drawn as disabled, and
        never taking the muted colour of the heading it sits in. With no
        filter on, it reads "Filter" in the theme's ordinary text colour,
        as any ghost button does. Hovering it, filters on or off, shows the
        usual hover over it. Clicking it opens a menu beneath it, one
        column, never wider than the sidebar, holding two sections, each
        headed by a small muted label, "Mode" and "Other mode", their
        options one per line, each with a checkbox, and the menu stays open
        while options are checked, closing on a click outside it or
        <Escape>. Each option reads its label and, muted after it, how many previous tasks match
        it on its own, whatever other options are checked; the latest task, heading
        the view, is never counted.

        The Mode section filters by the mode each task was sent in, one option
        per task mode in the @{PromptModes}'s order, "Code", "Chain",
        "Spec", and "Freeform", each with a small dot in its mode's colour
        before its label. The Other mode section filters by where a task stands with the other mode, as the
        @{PromptSending} says, with "Can send to Spec" for tasks that
        offer Send to Spec, "Can send to Code" for those that offer Send to
        Code, "Sent" for those already sent to the other mode or being sent
        there, and "Marked done" for those marked done by hand.

        With no option checked, which is how the list starts, every previous
        task shows. Within a section, the options checked combine as either, so a task
        shows when it matches any of them, and the two sections combine as
        both, so with options checked in each, a task shows only when it
        matches one in each. Clicking an option checks or unchecks it, the
        list filtering at once behind the open menu, and a "Clear filters"
        line at the menu's foot, shown only while any option is checked,
        unchecks them all. An option that would show no tasks is still
        listed, its count 0, and can still be checked.

        Filtering only hides tasks from the list; it changes nothing about
        them. A chain whose steps are hidden in part keeps its parent row
        above the steps still shown, and is hidden with its parent only when
        none of its steps shows. Day dividers show only above a day with a
        task shown, as the @{TasksTab} says. The row's count still counts every previous task,
        and while any option is checked, the Previous group's heading, after
        its count, reads " · N shown", N counting the previous tasks the
        filters show. A hidden task
        stays selected if it was, and is still counted in "N selected" and
        sent by the batch actions; an open task that is filtered out closes.
        With every task filtered out, the list reads "No previous tasks match
        these filters", muted, centred. The filters are kept while the list is
        closed and opened again, and each open project keeps its own, as the
        @{OpenProjects} says, but they are not saved with the project.

    chainedTasks:
        A prompt sent in Chain runs as steps, each saved in the history as a
        task of its own: first the Chain task itself, which writes the spec;
        then the same prompt sent to Code, told what the spec step did; and,
        when the chain asks for a post-build spec update, the same prompt sent
        to Spec once more, told what the code step did. Each step knows the
        one it was sent from, as the @{PromptSending} says. Only the
        steps the chain sends itself belong to it; a task sent to the other
        mode by hand, or resent, from one of its steps is a task of its own.

        Each step runs in the lane of the work it does, as the
        @{PromptSending} says: the Chain task and its spec follow-up in
        the spec lane, the code step in the code lane. A step is sent once the
        step before it finishes, never before, and takes its place in its
        lane's queue by when its chain was sent, not when the step itself was:
        behind every prompt waiting there that was sent before its chain,
        earlier chains' steps included, and ahead of every prompt sent after
        it. So a chain's code step never jumps ahead of a Code prompt, or
        another chain's code step, sent before the chain was, and a Code
        prompt sent while the chain's spec step ran waits for the chain's code
        step rather than coming between the chain's steps. Two steps whose
        chains were sent in the same order run in that order, whichever spec
        step finished first. Where nothing sent before its chain is waiting,
        it runs as soon as its lane is free, and straight away if it already
        is. Its place holds however it came to be queued, so a restart, with
        the queue read back from the project, keeps it there. The
        lane it leaves is free for its own queue meanwhile, so a Spec task
        sent while a chain's code step runs runs beside it. A step waiting for
        its lane shows in the queue like any prompt, and can be cancelled
        there, ending the chain. A step whose chain step before it failed or
        was cancelled is never sent.

        Each step also runs under the sandbox of the work it does, as the
        @{Sandbox} says: the spec steps under the Spec profile, which can't
        reach the code, and the code step under the Code profile, which can't
        reach the spec's source. Each carries on its own
        lane's conversation, as the @{HarnessIntegration} says, so a step
        is told what the step before it did only through its prompt: the
        Chain prompt as typed and that step's final output, as the
        @{ModeSystemPrompts} says.

        In the Tasks tab, a chain is one row, as the @{TasksTab} says,
        holding its steps, so they read as parts of a single chain: a joined
        chain icon, the first line of the prompt, and at its right how many
        steps it has, "2 steps" or "3 steps". Its status is
        the chain's as a whole, worked out only from its steps as they
        actually are, never from what the chain might still do: Running,
        with its spinner and its time counting, while one of its steps is
        building, compiling, or running now, in this project's runs, and
        while one of its steps waits in a lane's queue after an earlier step
        of it has run, as its code step queued behind another task once its
        spec step is done: the chain is under way, though that step is
        queued. It reads Running too between its steps, while a step of it
        has ended Done and the application is still to send the next, in
        this session: building the spec on the host after its spec step,
        compiling the next step, or waiting for that step's name, so it
        never reads Done for even a moment while a step it will run is still
        to come. A multi-step task is Done only once every step it was to
        run is Done; until then, a step of it Done is only that step's
        status, never the chain's. It reads Queued, in the theme's muted colour, only while it
        hasn't started, its first step still waiting in the queue and none
        of its steps run yet. Otherwise it is over: Failed or Cancelled if
        a step was; Done only where every step it was to run is Done; Not
        recorded where a step has no record of its run; and, where it
        stopped short of a step it was to run, with no step failed or
        cancelled, as when the application closed between its steps,
        Stopped, in the theme's muted colour, never Done. A chain whose later
        step was never sent, as one from before chains sent them or one
        whose application closed between its steps, is over, reading
        Stopped, its parent saying muted after its step count that its code
        step was never sent, and never reads Running or Done for it. What the
        application is still to send is known only in the session that sent
        the chain: after a restart, a chain whose next step wasn't sent or
        queued has stopped.

        Each step's state is read from where it truly is: a step is running
        only while the application has its run under way, which no step
        from the history ever does after a restart, since every run ends
        when the application quits; queued only while its prompt is in the
        queue; and otherwise from its own history record, a step with no
        record of its run reading Not recorded, never Running. Steps are
        joined to their chain only by which step each was sent from, as
        below, so a task never takes the state of a chain it doesn't belong
        to, and a chain never counts a task that isn't one of its steps.

        While a chain is Running, it is listed in the Running group of the
        right sidebar's Tasks tab, expanded, as the @{TasksTab} says; while
        Queued, in the Queued group; and once over, among the previous
        A chain with a step paused and none running reads Paused, and is
        listed with the running tasks, expanded, until it is resumed or
        cancelled.

        tasks. It has
        no checkbox, Resend, or Send to the other mode of its own, and
        clicking it doesn't open anything.

        In the right sidebar's Tasks tab, a chain is one row, as the
        @{TasksTab} says, never expanded in the list. Its steps are
        shown once it is opened, each as an item of its own, with its
        status, Resend, and whatever else a task offers, opening onto its
        own prompt, table, and changed files, labelled by what it did,
        "Spec", "Code", or "Spec follow-up", in that step's mode colour,
        listed in the order they ran. Selecting a chain's row selects its
        steps for the batch actions.

        A chain is listed where its first step was sent, oldest first like
        every other item; tasks sent while it ran, such as questions or tasks
        of the other lane run between its steps, are never listed within it,
        and are listed after the chain, in the order they were sent. A chain's
        steps are found by which step each was sent from, not by being next to
        each other in the history. A chain still running shows every step it
        will run from the moment it is sent, not only those sent so far: its
        spec step and its code step, and any step added to it, those not
        sent yet as steps to come, and a spec follow-up once the chain asks
        for one. A chain that is over shows the steps it sent, and a step
        still running shows as it would anywhere. When the latest
        task, heading the view, is a step of a chain, that chain's earlier
        steps are still grouped under its parent in the list, the parent
        saying the rest is the latest task. A chain is listed under the day its first
        step was sent, whatever day its later steps ran. A step
        from the history whose chain can't be worked out, as when the step
        it was sent from is missing, is listed as a task of its own.

    header:
        The latest task heads the view whether or not the harness is working.
        It is always the most recent task still active: of the tasks under
        way, building, compiling, or running, in either lane, the one sent
        most recently. Only while no task is under way is it the task that
        finished most recently, or, before any has finished, the one sent
        most recently. A finished task never heads the view while another is
        under way: when it finishes while a task in the other lane still
        runs, that task heads the view in its place at once, and the task
        that finished goes among the previous tasks. So with two Chain tasks
        sent one after the other, once both their spec steps have finished,
        the first chain's code step heads the view while it runs, not the
        second chain's finished spec step, and the second chain's code step
        heads it in turn once it is sent. A task still running in the other
        lane that is not the latest is a compact row in the header and in
        the Tasks tab's Running section, never among the previous tasks. Whichever task heads the
        view, its header and output show as they do for a task just sent,
        and the previous tasks, their row's count, and the chain grouping
        follow it. The header shows
        a single slim line, as the @{MessageList}'s sent prompt says: a bar
        in its mode's colour, its status and time, its summary, and a Stop
        and a View all button, never the prompt itself or the compiled
        markdown, which are seen in the Tasks tab. Messages sent to it while
        it runs are shown in its output.

    rawPrompt:
        Beside the latest task's Resend button, a small ghost button with a
        code icon, whose tooltip says "View the raw prompt", opens a modal
        showing exactly what the harness was given for the task, once it has
        been sent: nothing rendered, nothing hidden. Clicking it never opens or
        closes anything else in the header. Until the task has compiled, it
        is disabled, its tooltip reading "No prompt sent yet". Only the
        latest task's header has it; previous tasks and questions don't.

        The modal is titled "Raw prompt", with the task's mode, its hidden
        anchor's name, and the harness it was sent to beneath the title, or
        "Harness not recorded" for a task saved before the harness was. The
        anchor's name is shown for a Freeform task too, though its header
        hides it. It shows two sections, each headed and each with a Copy
        button that copies that section's whole text, disabled while the
        section has none to copy:

        \\
        - System prompt: the system prompt as the harness received it, with
          every placeholder filled in and every injected prompt in its place,
          the spec-reading prompt, and the code-to-spec prompt with the code
          task's prompt and final output, as the
          @{ModeSystemPrompts} says; or "No system prompt" when none was
          sent, as for Freeform.
        - User prompt: the compiled prompt the harness received, with its spec
          slices when it was sent with them.
        \\

        For a harness that takes no system prompt of its own, where it is given
        ahead of the prompt, as the @{HarnessIntegration} says, the modal
        shows the one text it was given, headed "Prompt", with a single Copy
        button. A prompt sent with attached images ends with a third section,
        "Attached images", listing the path of each saved image, one to a
        line, with its own Copy button. Messages sent to the task while it ran
        are not part of it; they are shown in its output.

        The text is shown as plain monospace text, wrapped, selectable, and
        scrolling within the modal, laying out only what is in view however
        long it is: it is drawn in rows of 16 lines, each laid out as it
        scrolls into view, so a selection is made within one row and can't be
        dragged across rows, while Copy always takes the whole section. The
        modal is 85% of the window's height, and 90% of its width up to 1000
        pixels. Pressing Escape,
        clicking outside it, or its close button closes it.

        What it shows is what was sent, not what the templates say now: the
        system prompt as sent, and the harness it was sent to, are saved in the
        task's history record beside its compiled user prompt, for questions as
        much as tasks, and for a harness given one combined text, the system
        prompt alone, the combined text built again from it exactly as it was
        sent, so the modal shows the same for a task restored
        from the history. A task saved before this was recorded shows its
        system prompt as "Not recorded".

    queue: @{QueueList}

    projects:
        Tasks, the queue, and the auto-send switch belong to their project, as
        the @{OpenProjects} says: the view shows the tasks and queue of the
        project on screen, while those of other open projects carry on in the
        background. A project's queues only ever send into that project.

    buildingTheSpec:
        A task builds the spec before it is sent, as the @{PromptModes}
        says, since the task may be the one to fix the spec, its output
        starting with an error saying the build failed when it does. A Spec
        task, or a Chain prompt's spec step, builds it again on the host once
        it is over, as the @{PromptSending} says, so the next step, and
        every task after it, reads the spec as that run left it.

    history:
        Every task sent is saved in the prompt history with the project's data,
        as the @{ProjectData} says, and its record once its run is over.
        Opening the project, the first time in a launch, brings back every
        task in order, with its output
        replayed from that record. The record keeps each message sent to the
        task while it ran, as typed and as compiled, in its place among the
        lines the harness printed, so the task comes back as the exchange it
        was, each message a Sent row followed by what came of it. A task with no record shows as
        "Not recorded".

        Every question asked from the Ask tab is logged the same way, but among
        the questions instead, so it never comes back as a task: it comes back
        in the @{AskConversation}. The record is saved when the run is
        over, and also when the question is stopped while it runs, with as
        much as the harness had done. A question
        that fails before it reaches the harness is logged too, with the error
        in its record.

    chatInput: @{ChatInput}

